00:04:31.350 --> 00:04:39.625
[SOUND].

00:04:39.625 --> 00:04:42.447
&gt;&gt; So, how does everyone think Scott did?

00:04:42.447 --> 00:04:43.770
That was pretty terrible, right?

00:04:44.940 --> 00:04:47.110
Scott, you're fired.

00:04:47.110 --> 00:04:50.830
So, why are we such bad interviewers?

00:04:52.280 --> 00:04:54.910
Given the social nature of interviews,

00:04:54.910 --> 00:04:58.130
there's often significant room for
interpretation.

00:04:59.880 --> 00:05:03.620
But it is not just intentional favoritism
or stereotyping.

00:05:04.850 --> 00:05:10.750
It is instead more often, one of the many
unconscious cognitive biases

00:05:10.750 --> 00:05:15.340
that can influence our ability to judge
talent and to hire effectively.

00:05:16.990 --> 00:05:21.410
So for instance, when Scott said you
graduated from the GSB,

00:05:21.410 --> 00:05:23.740
nice me too, how's your golf game.

00:05:24.760 --> 00:05:29.580
This is an example of similar to me bias
in which we develop an affinity for

00:05:29.580 --> 00:05:34.060
a candidate because he or she shares
personal characteristics with us.

00:05:35.420 --> 00:05:39.440
So instead of focusing on the
qualifications of the job at hand,

00:05:39.440 --> 00:05:43.194
we instead focus on shared background or
shared schooling or

00:05:43.194 --> 00:05:45.630
the fact that we root for the same sports
team.

00:05:47.560 --> 00:05:51.980
And similar me bias can also go hand in
hand with first impression error.

00:05:51.980 --> 00:05:54.810
As we heard from the first skit, first
impressions matter.

00:05:55.990 --> 00:06:00.600
So what happens here is our initial snap
judgements in an interview can set

00:06:00.600 --> 00:06:05.040
the tone and color the rest of the
interview time.

00:06:05.040 --> 00:06:08.820
So no matter what Andre said, in the next
29 minutes,

00:06:08.820 --> 00:06:14.590
those first few instances can affect how
Scott reads whatever he says thereafter.

00:06:17.240 --> 00:06:20.690
And these two biases can go hand in hand
with confirmation bias.

00:06:20.690 --> 00:06:23.810
So confirmation bias can occur when we

00:06:23.810 --> 00:06:29.280
seek information that reconfirms our
preexisting notions about an individual.

00:06:30.700 --> 00:06:34.810
And this can often lead to inconsistency
in questioning.

00:06:34.810 --> 00:06:37.960
When we treat different candidates
differently and

00:06:37.960 --> 00:06:39.089
ask them different questions.

00:06:40.630 --> 00:06:45.260
So we see this very clearly when Scott
said, I usually ask candidates about their

00:06:45.260 --> 00:06:48.250
modeling abilities, but given your
background, I think that's unnecessary.

00:06:49.700 --> 00:06:53.831
So Andre get to pass whereas all of those
other candidates get hammered on

00:06:53.831 --> 00:06:57.840
question after question, after question on
analysis and modeling.

00:06:59.620 --> 00:07:00.130
This is unfair.

00:07:01.670 --> 00:07:06.800
Yet despite all of these biases we don't
realize our judgement is so bad.

00:07:07.920 --> 00:07:09.030
Why is this?

00:07:09.030 --> 00:07:11.990
Yet another bias, over confidence bias.

00:07:13.340 --> 00:07:17.170
Over confidence bias occurs because we all
think that we

00:07:17.170 --> 00:07:20.280
have better than average judgement and
better than average intuition and

00:07:20.280 --> 00:07:23.210
that we're better than average
interviewers.

00:07:23.210 --> 00:07:27.420
So therefore, we don't even question what
we think when we

00:07:27.420 --> 00:07:28.620
make judgements about a candidate.

00:07:29.980 --> 00:07:33.440
This we saw again in Scott's over
confidence when

00:07:33.440 --> 00:07:38.200
before entering interview room he said,
well I'm really great at reading people so

00:07:38.200 --> 00:07:39.180
I don't need to read this resume.

00:07:42.020 --> 00:07:45.480
However, these biases really matter.

00:07:45.480 --> 00:07:47.290
A couple of small examples.

00:07:47.290 --> 00:07:53.270
One, it has been estimated that over 30%
of the gain in female orchestra

00:07:53.270 --> 00:07:58.630
members over a 20-year period has been
attributed to the use of blind auditions,

00:07:58.630 --> 00:08:02.790
in which candidates play behind a curtain.

00:08:04.170 --> 00:08:08.860
And even further, some researchers ask
that over 35% of

00:08:08.860 --> 00:08:13.590
hiring choice variation can be explained
by a candidate's obesity.

00:08:14.810 --> 00:08:17.680
So we can see that these biases really
matter.

00:08:18.930 --> 00:08:21.730
So now I'm gonna turn it over to Jorge to
explain how we

00:08:21.730 --> 00:08:23.220
can become better interviewers, and

00:08:23.220 --> 00:08:27.385
how we can use an interview process to
overcome these unconscious biases.

00:08:27.385 --> 00:08:31.415
[SOUND].

00:08:31.415 --> 00:08:33.740
&gt;&gt; So how does anybody become a better
interviewer?

00:08:35.640 --> 00:08:38.060
As with all forms of strategic
communication,

00:08:38.060 --> 00:08:39.790
the answer lies in preparation.

00:08:41.350 --> 00:08:45.720
I'm going to share with you today three
steps which anybody can use to

00:08:45.720 --> 00:08:49.430
outline a process that is far more
efficient than a lot of the,

00:08:49.430 --> 00:08:51.650
that what we've been talking about here
today.

00:08:51.650 --> 00:08:54.610
And actually selecting the right people
for the job.

00:08:56.110 --> 00:09:00.640
First step involves thinking carefully
about the person you're gonna hire and

00:09:00.640 --> 00:09:02.290
about their job profile.

00:09:02.290 --> 00:09:06.290
Job profile, think about it as a picture,
the ideal portrait of what this candidate

00:09:06.290 --> 00:09:09.340
is going to look like, or must look like
to fulfill the needs of the position.

00:09:09.340 --> 00:09:11.570
And in this picture, I can switch.

00:09:11.570 --> 00:09:14.910
You can then compare every one of your
candidates, and

00:09:14.910 --> 00:09:17.080
ensure that you have the best fit
possible.

00:09:18.810 --> 00:09:25.000
This portrait, should be built around both
background factors and personal factors.

00:09:25.000 --> 00:09:27.970
Both of these are intricately linked to
the next steps of the process so

00:09:27.970 --> 00:09:30.390
I will discuss them in more detail in just
a second.

00:09:32.370 --> 00:09:37.336
The second step is to select for resumes
that meet these requirements.

00:09:37.336 --> 00:09:41.330
Now the resume selecting, selection stage
is the gate to the process, and

00:09:41.330 --> 00:09:46.020
this is where you want to really focus on
not letting commonalities or

00:09:46.020 --> 00:09:49.340
your gut feeling get in the way to allow
them through the door.

00:09:49.340 --> 00:09:52.456
You, you, you have to focus on the
education and the experience

00:09:52.456 --> 00:09:55.880
requirements that are required of a person
to get to get this job.

00:09:57.440 --> 00:09:59.840
You need a rocket scientist, this is an
extreme example, but

00:09:59.840 --> 00:10:03.320
you of course need somebody, probably with
a degree in fluid mechanics, or

00:10:03.320 --> 00:10:05.250
mechanical engineering, and a PhD, for
example.

00:10:07.290 --> 00:10:11.460
Now, once you make your selection, you go
into the last step before the interview,

00:10:11.460 --> 00:10:13.640
which is to devise a personalized
interview plan.

00:10:13.640 --> 00:10:16.650
I call it personalized because you need to
focus here on the personal

00:10:16.650 --> 00:10:18.980
factors of the fir, of the candidates.

00:10:18.980 --> 00:10:20.950
These include, intellectual ability.

00:10:21.970 --> 00:10:24.220
It has to do with creative pow, creative
prowess.

00:10:24.220 --> 00:10:27.520
Analytical ability is your decision-making
process.

00:10:27.520 --> 00:10:29.450
You have to include personality.

00:10:30.960 --> 00:10:33.410
How dominant is this person, are they
extroverted?

00:10:34.680 --> 00:10:36.280
Are they patient and detail-oriented?

00:10:36.280 --> 00:10:38.840
These are [UNKNOWN] important things to
think about.

00:10:38.840 --> 00:10:41.280
Finally, motivation is incredibly
important.

00:10:42.560 --> 00:10:44.520
This might seem like common sense to you,
guys, and

00:10:44.520 --> 00:10:47.750
you don't need to be a fan of the show
Silicon Valley

00:10:47.750 --> 00:10:52.910
to understand the differences between
hiring a CEO and a coder.

00:10:52.910 --> 00:10:55.670
But you'd, you'd be surprised to see how
many people don't really

00:10:55.670 --> 00:10:58.150
think carefully about these poor engaging
candidates and interviews.

00:10:59.680 --> 00:11:04.070
Now it's very important that you actually
are focusing on the positions throughout

00:11:04.070 --> 00:11:08.190
these whole process and not your personal
prod, preferences or ideas.

00:11:09.610 --> 00:11:10.530
Now we're at the interview.

00:11:12.570 --> 00:11:16.010
It's very easy once you get to the
interview to, and you have a resume in

00:11:16.010 --> 00:11:19.020
front of you, to let that sort of take
control and be your agenda.

00:11:19.020 --> 00:11:20.060
Do not let that happen.

00:11:20.060 --> 00:11:24.280
You need to come into this interview with
a set of topics that you're gonna use as

00:11:24.280 --> 00:11:29.450
your agenda, and these topics, the, the
details are not really that important.

00:11:29.450 --> 00:11:30.588
Doesn't matter what the topic is.

00:11:30.588 --> 00:11:34.350
What you wanna use this topic for is to
open up the conversation.

00:11:34.350 --> 00:11:38.160
These topic openers will then give you
information about the candidate,

00:11:38.160 --> 00:11:41.110
the specifics about where they work, who
they work with,

00:11:41.110 --> 00:11:44.060
what specific project they worked on, or
where they were educated.

00:11:45.220 --> 00:11:48.670
And here, that's where you launch the, the
good questions.

00:11:48.670 --> 00:11:50.184
This is what the real interview is about.

00:11:50.184 --> 00:11:56.040
You wanna focus on getting in delving deep
into the person that,

00:11:56.040 --> 00:11:58.289
into this person and, and their personal
factors

00:11:59.870 --> 00:12:04.101
through probing questions either
self-appraisal or situation-based.

00:12:05.210 --> 00:12:09.050
As an example, you can ask a question such
as,

00:12:09.050 --> 00:12:13.380
tell me about a time when you had to make
a decision in a difficult situation for

00:12:13.380 --> 00:12:15.220
there was no, for which there was no
policy.

00:12:16.390 --> 00:12:18.720
Now these kinds of questions shed light
into their analytical, or

00:12:18.720 --> 00:12:21.930
their intellectual ability, and their
personality.

00:12:21.930 --> 00:12:25.500
And to avoid people falling back on
pre-canned answers,

00:12:25.500 --> 00:12:27.760
you have to delve deeper into those
questions.

00:12:27.760 --> 00:12:28.980
How did the people around you react?

00:12:32.060 --> 00:12:35.700
How did you overcome those that were
against whatever decision you made?

00:12:35.700 --> 00:12:37.950
And from these you can make your own
conclusions, and

00:12:37.950 --> 00:12:39.220
you can repeat the process over and

00:12:39.220 --> 00:12:42.340
over again until you you have all the
information you need to ensure that you

00:12:42.340 --> 00:12:45.120
have a fit of the candidates to the
portrait.

00:12:45.120 --> 00:12:47.555
Speaking of conclusions, I will get over
to Andre now.

00:12:47.555 --> 00:12:50.947
[SOUND].

00:12:50.947 --> 00:12:55.345
&gt;&gt; There is nothing more important than
getting the rock stars on

00:12:55.345 --> 00:13:01.110
the rocketship that you want your company
to be, the best athletes in your team.

00:13:02.400 --> 00:13:06.610
Interviewing is the way to get those
people into the fold.

00:13:06.610 --> 00:13:08.670
And yet, so often we're bad at it.

00:13:08.670 --> 00:13:12.190
We're bad at it because, as you've heard,
we don't practice,

00:13:12.190 --> 00:13:17.170
we don't lift the weights before the race,
eat our proteins, practice on the track.

00:13:18.300 --> 00:13:19.849
So we'd like to leave you with one
message.

00:13:20.910 --> 00:13:23.526
Do the practice, do the training, and

00:13:23.526 --> 00:13:26.950
bring those, those star athletes into your
team.

00:13:26.950 --> 00:13:27.550
Thank you.

00:13:27.550 --> 00:13:33.229
